The publish gate. While this file exists the release workflow tags nothing.

yze-md-hardwrap has never been pushed, has no remote, and has no tags — and
that is the point: creating the repository and publishing the module are the
owner's decisions, not a side effect of a commit. proxy.golang.org is
first-write-wins and permanent, so the first fetch of any version caches that
tree publicly forever and sum.golang.org records its hash; nothing about that
is undone by deleting a repository.

So the hold stays until publication is deliberate: the leak vet passes over the
whole tree and its full history, an adversarial round returns clean, the
analyzer is wired into the stickler configuration and pinned in tools.txt, and
only then is a single v0.1.0 cut from a tree somebody chose.

Removing this file IS the act of publishing. Do not remove it to quiet a
workflow.
